// Gen9 scene (op 6): the fill and the tone records only, drawn in order over one 16-byte block (four pixels) of one row. Each
// thread is one block: its group X (r0.1) and row Y (r0.6) from the walker, at the pixel x = px0 + 4 X and y = py0 + Y (CURBE
// dwords 1 and 2; CURBE dword 0 is the record count). The block is read on binding table entry 0 and written back on it after
// every record, in order, as the processor's scene draws them. Binding table entry 1 is the records: count of them, 32 bytes
// each, two a row of a surface 64 bytes a row (pitch 64; a pitch of 32 reads its odd rows as zeros on the card, measured on
// subtle-cottage): record k is a media block read of 32 bytes at x 32 (k & 1), row k >> 1. Its words: 0 kind (0 a fill, 1
// a tone over), 1 x0, 2 y0, 3 x1, 4 y1 (the rectangle [x0, x1) by [y0, y1) in pixels), 5 the colour.
//
// Per record, for the four pixels (exec size 4): a fill writes the colour where the pixel is in the rectangle; a tone lays the
// colour over the pixel as ui_over does (tone.iga's formula, on the running pixel) where the pixel is in the rectangle and the
// colour's alpha is not 0 (an alpha of 0 leaves the pixel). The rectangle test is on sign bits: x - x0, x1 - 1 - x and the row's
// terms are OR-ed, so a term is negative exactly when the pixel is outside, and one signed compare with 0 per lane sets the
// flags f0.0 (the fill) and f1.0 (the tone). f0.1 is the loop's flag, compared at SIMD16 with its scalars broadcast, so every
// channel of the jump's predicate agrees. Lane ids 0..3 are four scalar movs. The encoder takes only records of kind 0 or 1, a count from 1 to 16384, and the alignments the card takes; anything else is the processor's.
